# The rules of the API role's Valkey user, for a server run as two processes
# (RUNTARA_ROLE, docs/roles.md). One rule a line; a line that starts with #
# is a comment. Apply them with the user's name and password:
#
#   valkey-cli ACL SETUSER runtara-api on '><password>' \
#       $(grep -v '^#' scripts/roles/api-valkey-user.acl)
#
# and give the API VALKEY_USER and VALKEY_PASSWORD. The engine's user keeps
# every key.
#
# The API reads who is a member and which tokens are revoked, counts its
# tenants' quotas, keeps MCP sessions and session queues, writes product
# events, and reads the control stream. It cannot write a trigger stream
# (runtara:triggers:*), a compilation queue or its progress
# (*:compilation:*), an admission count (*:admission:*), or add to the
# control stream.
resetkeys
resetchannels
-@all
# Who is a member, which tokens are revoked, whether a tenant is ready.
%R~*:member:*
%R~member:*
~*:token:revoked:*
~token:revoked:*
%R~runtara:tenant_status:*
# Quotas the API counts itself.
~*:quota:api
~*:quota:compile
# MCP sessions, session queues and their index.
~runtara:mcp:sessions:*
~queue:*
~runtara:session:*
~*:session:*
# Product events. A stream key of another name
# (RUNTARA_PRODUCT_EVENTS_STREAM_KEY) is added here.
~runtara:product_events:*
+@all
-@admin
-@dangerous
+info
# The control stream is read through a consumer group, and never added to.
(%RW~*:control resetchannels -@all +xgroup +xreadgroup +xack +xautoclaim +xpending +xinfo +xlen +ping)
